## ASSEMBLED SECTION



### <img src="https://flagcdn.com/fr.svg" width="32" alt="France" style={{display: 'inline', verticalAlign: 'middle', position: 'relative', top: '-2px', marginRight: '8px', border: '1px solid #d1d5db', borderRadius: '2px'}} />France

France continues navigating structural governance instability while maintaining international positioning and defense expansion. Domestically, the July 7, 2026 Le Pen appeal verdict date is now confirmed as the pivotal binary event for 2027 electoral dynamics, while institutional strain manifests through contested appointments like Montchalin to Cour des comptes amid 'verrouillage' accusations. Political violence in Lyon indicates rising tensions ahead of 2027. Internationally, France maintains diplomatic and institutional engagement despite scandal management (Epstein-linked diplomat) and continues defense capability demonstration through Orion 26—its largest military exercise since the Cold War. Economic posture remains defensive with strategic institutional maneuvering, notably Villeroy de Galhau's early central bank departure to prevent potential RN capture. The tension persists between European leadership ambitions and domestic fragmentation, now with confirmed timeline for the binary electoral event that could fundamentally alter France's trajectory.

**Key developments:**

- **Domestic:** Paris appeals court concluded Marine Le Pen's embezzlement trial and announced verdict will be delivered July 7, 2026. The decision will determine whether Le Pen can run for president in 2027 or remains barred by the five-year ban sought by prosecutors. This confirms the timing of the pivotal binary event for 2027 electoral dynamics.
- **Domestic:** President Macron nominated Budget Minister Amélie de Montchalin to head the Cour des comptes. Opposition parties criticized this as part of a 'verrouillage' strategy to place loyalists in key institutions before 2027 election. Magistrates questioned how she can audit budgets she designed.
- **Domestic:** 23-year-old nationalist militant Quentin Deranque died after violent assault outside LFI conference by Rima Hassan. Right-wing politicians accused LFI and antifascist groups of involvement, with connections drawn to Jeune Garde collective linked to LFI deputy Raphaël Arnault. Political class unanimously condemned violence while partisan blame intensified.
- **Diplomatic:** French diplomat Fabrice Aidan exchanged extensive emails with Jeffrey Epstein between 2010-2017 and was investigated by FBI in 2013 for suspected consultation of pedophile content. Foreign Minister Barrot expressed being 'appalled' and referred case to French prosecutors. Aidan has been suspended from his Engie position while remaining on Quai d'Orsay rolls.
- **Security:** France began its largest military exercise since the Cold War mobilizing 12,500 troops, 25 ships including Charles de Gaulle carrier, 140 aircraft, and 1,200 drones across Atlantic coast through April. Exercise simulates high-intensity conflict scenarios with allied participation. Armed Forces Ministry described it as 'without precedent.'

<Accordion title="Other Stories">
- **Marine Le Pen appeal trial concludes with verdict scheduled for July 7, determining her 2027 presidential eligibility** — The Paris appeals court concluded Marine Le Pen's trial on February 11 and announced the verdict will be delivered July 7, 2026. The decision will determine whether Le Pen can run for president in 2027 or remains ineligible due to the five-year ban sought by prosecutors. *([ouest-france.fr](https://ouest-france.fr/politique/rassemblement-national/direct-proces-en-appel-du-rn-dernier-jour-dune-audience-decisive-les-avocats-de-marine-le-pen-plaident-ce-mercredi-37056876-b489-4df3-888e-923003123cd2))*
- **Emmanuel Macron appoints Amélie de Montchalin to head Cour des comptes amid accusations of 'verrouillage' strategy** — President Macron announced the nomination of Budget Minister Amélie de Montchalin to lead the Cour des comptes (Court of Auditors). Opposition parties criticized the appointment as part of a broader strategy to place loyalists in key institutions before the 2027 election. *([huffingtonpost.fr](https://lemonde.fr/politique/article/2026/02/12/emmanuel-macron-en-nommant-des-proches-a-des-postes-cles-est-accuse-de-fragiliser-les-institutions_6666461_823448.html))*
- **Prime Minister Lecornu unveils new energy strategy (PPE 3) prioritizing nuclear power and offshore wind** — Prime Minister Sébastien Lecornu announced France's new energy roadmap during a visit to Jura on February 12, emphasizing nuclear reactor construction and offshore wind development. The strategy faced criticism from Marine Le Pen who called the decree publication a 'serious mistake.' *([france3-regions.franceinfo.fr](https://www.lemonde.fr/economie/article/2026/02/12/strategie-energetique-le-gouvernement-donne-la-priorite-au-nucleaire_6666442_3234.html))*
- **French diplomat Fabrice Aidan cited in Epstein files, Quai d'Orsay launches investigation** — Radio France revealed that French diplomat Fabrice Aidan exchanged numerous emails with Jeffrey Epstein between 2010-2017 and was previously investigated by the FBI in 2013 for alleged pedophile content consultation. Foreign Minister Jean-Noël Barrot said he was 'appalled' and referred the case to French prosecutors. *([franceinfo.fr](https://www.mediapart.fr/journal/international/100226/hold-contradictoire-jeffrey-epstein-le-fbi-et-le-diplomate-francais-l-enquete-qui-fait-trembler-le))*
- **Jordan Bardella effigy burned at Basque carnival, prompting investigation** — A large effigy of RN President Jordan Bardella was burned during the traditional Zan Pantzar carnival in Hasparren (Pyrénées-Atlantiques) on February 7. The Bayonne prosecutor's office opened an investigation on February 13 into the incident, which was filmed and shared on social media. *([franceinfo.fr](https://franceinfo.fr/politique/jordan-bardella/une-poupee-geante-a-l-effigie-de-jordan-bardella-brulee-lors-d-un-carnaval-une-enquete-ouverte_7803170.html))*
